{
  "quikchat-backend/.env.example": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/.env.example`\n- High-Level Purpose: Provides an example configuration file for environment variables used by the QuikChat backend application, detailing essential settings for database, Redis, JWT, and application behavior.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Variables / Constants**:\n    - `PORT`: (int) The port on which the server listens (default: 8080).\n    - `DATABASE_URL`: (string) Connection string for the PostgreSQL database.\n    - `REDIS_ADDRESS`: (string) Address for the Redis server.\n    - `JWT_SECRET_KEY`: (string) Secret key used for signing and verifying JSON Web Tokens.\n    - `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRY`: (duration) Expiry time for access tokens (e.g., 15m).\n    - `REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRY`: (duration) Expiry time for refresh tokens (e.g., 72h).\n    - `MAX_GROUP_MEMBERS`: (int) Maximum number of members allowed in a chat group.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Environment Variable Configuration: Standard practice for managing application settings across different environments.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/.gitignore": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/.gitignore`\n- High-Level Purpose: Specifies intentionally untracked files and directories that Git should ignore, such as build artifacts, IDE settings, and environment files.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - This is a configuration file and does not contain code definitions.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Standard ignore patterns for Go projects, build outputs, IDE files, and sensitive environment variables.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/Dockerfile": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/Dockerfile`\n- High-Level Purpose: Defines a multi-stage Docker build process for the QuikChat backend application, including building frontend assets and packaging the backend binary with static files and migrations into a lightweight production image.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Stages**:\n    - `frontend-builder`: Uses `node:18-alpine` to build frontend assets.\n    - `backend-builder`: Uses `golang:1.21-alpine` to build the Go backend application and copy frontend assets.\n    - `alpine:latest`: The final production image, containing only the backend binary, static web assets, and migrations.\n  - **Commands**:\n    - `npm install`, `npm run build`: Frontend build commands.\n    - `go mod download`, `go build`: Backend build commands.\n    - `COPY --from=...`: Copies build artifacts between stages.\n    - `EXPOSE 8080`: Exposes the application's listening port.\n    - `CMD [\"./quikchat\"]`: Specifies the command to run the backend application.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Multi-stage Docker Build: Optimizes image size by separating build environments from the final runtime environment.\n  - Asset Bundling: Integrates frontend build into the backend Docker image.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/README.md": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/README.md`\n- High-Level Purpose: Provides an overview, architectural details, and instructions for setting up and running the QuikChat backend locally.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - This is a documentation file and does not contain code definitions.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Documentation: Describes the project's language, architecture, database, cache, API type, and local setup steps using Docker Compose.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/api/swagger.yaml": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/api/swagger.yaml`\n- High-Level Purpose: Defines the OpenAPI 3.0 specification for the QuikChat backend API, providing comprehensive documentation for all available endpoints, their request/response schemas, security requirements, and descriptions.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **API Information**:\n    - `title`: QuikChat API\n    - `version`: 1.0.0\n    - `description`: API for the QuikChat real-time messaging platform.\n  - **Servers**:\n    - `url: /api/v1`: Specifies the base path for all API endpoints.\n  - **Security Schemes**:\n    - `bearerAuth`: Defines a JWT-based HTTP Bearer authentication scheme.\n  - **Paths**:\n    - Detailed specifications for various API endpoints, including:\n      - User authentication (`/auth/register`, `/auth/login`).\n      - User profile management (`/users/me`, `/users/profile/{username}`, `/users/me/profile`).\n      - User blocking (`/users/block/{username}`, `/users/unblock/{username}`).\n      - Friend management (`/friends/requests`, `/friends/requests/{requestID}`, `/friends`, `/friends/{username}`).\n      - Group management (`/groups`, `/groups/search`, `/groups/{groupID}`, `/groups/{groupID}/join`, `/groups/{groupID}/leave`).\n      - Message and media (`/conversations/{conversationID}/messages`, `/media/upload-url`).\n      - Real-time WebSocket connection (`/ws`).\n      - Notification management (`/notifications`, `/notifications/unread-count`, `/notifications/read-all`, `/notifications/{notificationID}/read`).\n    - Each path specifies HTTP methods (POST, GET, PUT, DELETE), summary, tags, request bodies (with schema references), parameters (path, query), and responses (status codes, descriptions, content schemas).\n  - **Components/Schemas**:\n    - Reusable schema definitions for common data structures:\n      - `User`: User object (id, username, display_name, profile_image_url).\n      - `RegisterRequest`: Request for user registration (username, password).\n      - `LoginRequest`: Request for user login (username, password).\n      - `AuthResponse`: Response for successful login (access_token, refresh_token).\n      - `UpdateProfileRequest`: Request for updating user profile (display_name, profile_image_url).\n      - `FriendRequest`: Friend request object (id, sender_id, receiver_id, status).\n      - `CreateGroupRequest`: Request for creating a group (name, tag, description).\n      - `SendMessageRequest`: Request for sending a message (type, content).\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - OpenAPI Specification: Provides a standardized, machine-readable format for API documentation.\n  - RESTful API Design: Adheres to REST principles for resource-oriented endpoints and HTTP methods.\n  - JWT Authentication: Explicitly defines and applies `bearerAuth` for protected endpoints.\n  - Schema Definitions: Uses reusable component schemas for consistent data structures across the API.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/cmd/api/main.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/cmd/api/main.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: The main entry point for the QuikChat backend application. It orchestrates the initialization of all core components: logging, configuration, database connection, WebSocket hub, repositories, services (use cases), HTTP handlers, and the router, finally starting the HTTP server with graceful shutdown capabilities.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `main()`: The application's entry point.\n      - Initializes `slog` for structured logging.\n      - Loads application configuration using `config.Load()`.\n      - Establishes a connection to the PostgreSQL database using `pgxpool.New()`.\n      - Initializes and starts the WebSocket `hub` in a separate goroutine.\n      - Initializes concrete implementations of all `repository` interfaces (PostgreSQL-based).\n      - Initializes a `MockS3Client` (a placeholder for a real S3 client in production).\n      - Initializes concrete implementations of all `service` (use case) interfaces, injecting their required repository and external service dependencies.\n      - Initializes all HTTP `handler` instances, injecting their respective services and the WebSocket hub.\n      - Creates the main HTTP `router` using `router.New()`, passing in all handlers, the hub, and the JWT secret.\n      - Starts the HTTP server (`http.Server.ListenAndServe()`) in a goroutine.\n      - Implements graceful shutdown logic: listens for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` signals, then attempts to shut down the server cleanly within a 5-second timeout.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Dependency Injection: All components (repositories, services, handlers) are created and wired together in `main`, promoting loose coupling and testability.\n  - Layered Architecture: Clearly separates concerns into configuration, logging, database, WebSocket, data access (repositories), business logic (services/use cases), and presentation (HTTP handlers).\n  - Graceful Shutdown: Handles OS signals to allow the server to finish active requests before exiting, preventing abrupt termination.\n  - Concurrency: Runs the WebSocket hub and HTTP server in separate goroutines.\n  - Structured Logging: Uses `slog` for consistent, machine-readable log output.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/docker-compose.yml": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/docker-compose.yml`\n- High-Level Purpose: Defines a multi-service Docker Compose setup for the QuikChat application, orchestrating the backend, PostgreSQL database, and Redis cache for local development and deployment.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Services**:\n    - `backend`:\n      - Builds from the `Dockerfile` in the parent directory.\n      - Maps host port 8080 to container port 8080.\n      - Depends on `db` and `redis` services being healthy.\n      - Sets environment variables for configuration (PORT, DATABASE_URL, REDIS_ADDRESS, JWT_SECRET_KEY).\n      - Mounts backend and frontend source code as volumes for live reloading/development.\n    - `db`:\n      - Uses the `postgres:15-alpine` Docker image.\n      - Maps host port 5432 to container port 5432.\n      - Mounts a named volume `postgres_data` for persistent database storage.\n      - Configures PostgreSQL user, password, and database name.\n      - Includes a `healthcheck` using `pg_isready` to ensure the database is ready.\n    - `redis`:\n      - Uses the `redis:7-alpine` Docker image.\n      - Maps host port 6379 to container port 6379.\n      - Mounts a named volume `redis_data` for persistent Redis data.\n      - Includes a `healthcheck` using `redis-cli ping` to ensure Redis is ready.\n  - **Volumes**:\n    - `postgres_data`: Named volume for PostgreSQL data persistence.\n    - `redis_data`: Named volume for Redis data persistence.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Docker Compose: Orchestrates multiple containers for a complete application stack.\n  - Service Dependencies: Uses `depends_on` with `service_healthy` condition to manage startup order.\n  - Health Checks: Ensures services are fully operational before dependent services start.\n  - Volume Mounting: Persists database and Redis data, and facilitates development by mounting source code.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/go.mod": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/go.mod`\n- High-Level Purpose: Defines the Go module path for the QuikChat backend project and lists all direct and indirect external dependencies required for building and running the application.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Module**: `quikchat`\n  - **Go Version**: `1.21`\n  - **Direct Dependencies**:\n    - `github.com/go-chi/chi/v5`: A lightweight, idiomatic, and composable router for building HTTP services.\n    - `github.com/golang-jwt/jwt/v5`: A Go package for working with JSON Web Tokens (JWTs).\n    - `github.com/google/uuid`: A package for generating and parsing UUIDs.\n    - `github.com/gorilla/websocket`: A WebSocket implementation for Go.\n    - `github.com/jackc/pgx/v5`: A pure Go driver for PostgreSQL.\n    - `github.com/joho/godotenv`: A Go port of the Ruby dotenv library, for loading environment variables from `.env` files.\n    - `golang.org/x/crypto`: Go supplementary cryptography libraries, used for password hashing (bcrypt).\n    - `golang.org/x/time`: Go supplementary time libraries, used for rate limiting.\n  - **Indirect Dependencies**:\n    - Includes various sub-dependencies of `pgx/v5` (e.g., `pgpassfile`, `pgservicefile`, `puddle/v2`) and standard library extensions (e.g., `golang.org/x/net`, `golang.org/x/sync`, `golang.org/x/text`).\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Go Modules: Standard dependency management system for Go projects.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/external/s3/s3.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/external/s3/s3.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Defines an interface for S3-compatible object storage operations and provides a mock implementation for development.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Interfaces**:\n    - `S3Client`: Interface for S3 interactions.\n      - Methods: `GeneratePresignedUploadURL(ctx context.Context, bucket, key string, expiry time.Duration) (string, error)`, `GeneratePresignedDownloadURL(ctx context.Context, bucket, key string, expiry time.Duration) (string, error)`.\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `MockS3Client`: A placeholder implementation of `S3Client`.\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewMockS3Client() S3Client`: Public constructor for `MockS3Client`.\n    - `GeneratePresignedUploadURL(ctx context.Context, bucket, key string, expiry time.Duration) (string, error)`: Public method of `MockS3Client` that returns a fake upload URL.\n    - `GeneratePresignedDownloadURL(ctx context.Context, bucket, key string, expiry time.Duration) (string, error)`: Public method of `MockS3Client` that returns a fake download URL.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Interface-based Design: Defines a contract for S3 operations, allowing for different implementations (e.g., real AWS S3, mock).\n  - Mock Implementation: Provides a simple, non-functional implementation useful for testing and local development without actual S3 credentials.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/friend_handler.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/friend_handler.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements HTTP handlers for friend-related API endpoints, translating incoming HTTP requests into calls to the `FriendUseCase` and formatting HTTP responses.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `FriendHandler`: Holds a reference to the `FriendUseCase` for business logic execution.\n      - Fields: `friendService` (usecase.FriendUseCase).\n    - `SendFriendRequest`: Request body structure for sending a friend request.\n      - Fields: `Username` (string).\n    - `RespondToRequest`: Request body structure for responding to a friend request.\n      - Fields: `Action` (string, e.g., \"accept\", \"decline\").\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewFriendHandler(friendService usecase.FriendUseCase) *FriendHandler`: Public constructor for `FriendHandler`.\n    - `SendRequest(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for sending a friend request.\n    - `ListIncomingRequests(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for listing incoming friend requests.\n    - `RespondToRequest(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for responding to a specific friend request.\n    - `ListFriends(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for listing the current user's friends.\n    - `Unfriend(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for unfriending a user.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Handler: Implements `http.HandlerFunc` pattern.\n  - Request/Response Marshalling: Uses `json.NewDecoder` and `json.NewEncoder` for JSON payload handling.\n  - Error Handling: Returns appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages.\n  - Context Usage: Extracts `user_id` from the request context, typically set by authentication middleware.\n  - URL Parameters: Uses `chi.URLParam` to extract parameters like `requestID` and `username` from the URL path.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/group_handler.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/group_handler.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements HTTP handlers for group-related API endpoints, translating incoming HTTP requests into calls to the `GroupUseCase` and formatting HTTP responses.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `GroupHandler`: Holds a reference to the `GroupUseCase` for business logic execution.\n      - Fields: `groupService` (usecase.GroupUseCase).\n    - `CreateGroupRequest`: Request body structure for creating a group.\n      - Fields: `Name` (string), `Tag` (string), `Description` (*string).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewGroupHandler(groupService usecase.GroupUseCase) *GroupHandler`: Public constructor for `GroupHandler`.\n    - `CreateGroup(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for creating a new group. It extracts `user_id` from context, decodes the request body, calls `groupService.CreateGroup`, and returns the created group.\n    - `SearchGroups(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for searching groups by a `tag` query parameter. It calls `groupService.SearchGroups` and returns the results.\n    - `GetGroupDetails(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for retrieving details of a specific group and its members. It extracts `user_id` from context and `groupID` from URL parameters, calls `groupService.GetGroupDetails`, and returns the group and its members.\n    - `JoinGroup(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for a user to join a group. It extracts `user_id` from context and `groupID` from URL parameters, calls `groupService.JoinGroup`, and returns a success status.\n    - `LeaveGroup(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for a user to leave a group. It extracts `user_id` from context and `groupID` from URL parameters, calls `groupService.LeaveGroup`, and returns a success status.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Handler: Implements `http.HandlerFunc` pattern for REST endpoints.\n  - Request/Response Marshalling: Uses `json.NewDecoder` and `json.NewEncoder` for JSON payload handling.\n  - Error Handling: Returns appropriate HTTP status codes (e.g., `400 Bad Request`, `401 Unauthorized`, `404 Not Found`, `409 Conflict`, `500 Internal Server Error`) and error messages based on service layer errors.\n  - Context Usage: Extracts `user_id` from the request context, typically set by authentication middleware.\n  - URL Parameters: Uses `chi.URLParam` to extract `groupID`.\n  - Query Parameters: Uses `r.URL.Query().Get` for `tag`.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/message_handler.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/message_handler.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements HTTP handlers for message-related API endpoints and a WebSocket endpoint for real-time communication.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Variables**:\n    - `upgrader`: Module-level `websocket.Upgrader` configured to allow all origins (for development).\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `MessageHandler`: Holds references to the `MessageUseCase` and the WebSocket `Hub`.\n      - Fields: `messageService` (usecase.MessageUseCase), `hub` (*ws.Hub).\n    - `SendMessageRequest`: Request body structure for sending a message.\n      - Fields: `Type` (domain.MessageType), `Content` (string).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewMessageHandler(messageService usecase.MessageUseCase, hub *ws.Hub) *MessageHandler`: Public constructor for `MessageHandler`.\n    - `SendMessage(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to send a new message to a conversation.\n    - `GetMessages(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to retrieve messages for a specific conversation, supporting cursor-based pagination.\n    - `GetPresignedURL(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to generate a presigned URL for media uploads to S3.\n    - `ServeWs(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to upgrade an HTTP connection to a WebSocket connection, registering the client with the hub.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Handler: Implements `http.HandlerFunc` pattern for REST endpoints.\n  - WebSocket Endpoint: Handles WebSocket connection upgrades and client registration.\n  - Request/Response Marshalling: Uses `json.NewDecoder` and `json.NewEncoder` for JSON payload handling.\n  - Error Handling: Returns appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages.\n  - Context Usage: Extracts `user_id` from the request context.\n  - URL Parameters: Uses `chi.URLParam` to extract `conversationID`.\n  - Query Parameters: Uses `r.URL.Query().Get` for `cursor`, `limit`, and `filename`.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/notification_handler.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/notification_handler.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements HTTP handlers for notification-related API endpoints, translating incoming HTTP requests into calls to the `NotificationUseCase` and formatting HTTP responses.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `NotificationHandler`: Holds a reference to the `NotificationUseCase` for business logic execution.\n      - Fields: `notificationService` (usecase.NotificationUseCase).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewNotificationHandler(notificationService usecase.NotificationUseCase) *NotificationHandler`: Public constructor for `NotificationHandler`.\n    - `GetNotifications(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to retrieve a paginated list of notifications for the authenticated user. It extracts `user_id` from context and `limit`/`offset` from query parameters.\n    - `MarkAsRead(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to mark a specific notification as read. It extracts `user_id` from context and `notificationID` from the URL path.\n    - `MarkAllAsRead(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to mark all unread notifications for the authenticated user as read. It extracts `user_id` from context.\n    - `GetUnreadCount(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to retrieve the count of unread notifications for the authenticated user. It extracts `user_id` from context.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Handler: Implements `http.HandlerFunc` pattern for REST endpoints.\n  - Request/Response Marshalling: Uses `json.NewDecoder` and `json.NewEncoder` for JSON payload handling.\n  - Error Handling: Returns appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages.\n  - Context Usage: Extracts `user_id` from the request context, typically set by authentication middleware.\n  - URL/Query Parameters: Uses `chi.URLParam` and `r.URL.Query().Get` to extract request parameters.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/user_handler.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/handler/user_handler.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements HTTP handlers for user-related API endpoints, translating incoming HTTP requests into calls to the `UserUseCase` and formatting HTTP responses.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `UserHandler`: Holds a reference to the `UserUseCase` for business logic execution.\n      - Fields: `userService` (usecase.UserUseCase).\n    - `RegisterRequest`: Request body structure for user registration.\n      - Fields: `Username` (string), `Password` (string).\n    - `LoginRequest`: Request body structure for user login.\n      - Fields: `Username` (string), `Password` (string).\n    - `AuthResponse`: Response body structure for successful login, containing tokens.\n      - Fields: `AccessToken` (string), `RefreshToken` (string).\n    - `UpdateProfileRequest`: Request body structure for updating user profile.\n      - Fields: `DisplayName` (*string), `ProfileImageURL` (*string).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewUserHandler(userService usecase.UserUseCase) *UserHandler`: Public constructor for `UserHandler`.\n    - `Register(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for user registration.\n    - `Login(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler for user login.\n    - `GetCurrentUser(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to get the ID of the currently authenticated user.\n    - `GetProfileByUsername(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to retrieve a user's profile by username.\n    - `UpdateProfile(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to update the authenticated user's profile.\n    - `BlockUser(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to block another user.\n    - `UnblockUser(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request)`: Public HTTP handler to unblock another user.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Handler: Implements `http.HandlerFunc` pattern.\n  - Request/Response Marshalling: Uses `json.NewDecoder` and `json.NewEncoder` for JSON payload handling.\n  - Error Handling: Returns appropriate HTTP status codes and error messages.\n  - Context Usage: Extracts `user_id` from the request context, typically set by authentication middleware.\n  - URL Parameters: Uses `chi.URLParam` to extract parameters from the URL path.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/router/router.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/handler/http/router/router.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Sets up the HTTP routing for the QuikChat backend API using `go-chi/chi`, including global and route-specific middleware, public and protected API routes, and serving static frontend files.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `New(userHandler *handler.UserHandler, friendHandler *handler.FriendHandler, messageHandler *handler.MessageHandler, groupHandler *handler.GroupHandler, notificationHandler *handler.NotificationHandler, hub *ws.Hub, jwtSecret string) http.Handler`: Public constructor that initializes and returns a new `chi.Mux` (HTTP router). It applies global middleware (`RequestID`, `RealIP`, `StructuredLogger`, `Recoverer`), defines public authentication routes (`/auth/register`, `/auth/login`) with rate limiting, a WebSocket endpoint (`/ws`), and then protected API routes for users, friends, groups, messages, and notifications, all requiring JWT authentication.\n    - `FileServer(r chi.Router, path string, root http.FileSystem)`: Public helper function to serve static files from a given `http.FileSystem`. It includes logic for Single Page Application (SPA) routing (serving `index.html` for non-existent paths) and handles trailing slash redirects.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - HTTP Router: Uses `go-chi/chi` for declarative and modular routing.\n  - Middleware Chaining: Applies various middleware for logging, request ID, recovery, rate limiting, and authentication.\n  - API Versioning: All API routes are prefixed with `/api/v1`.\n  - Authentication Guard: `middleware.Auth` protects specific route groups, ensuring JWT validation.\n  - SPA Static File Serving: Configured to serve frontend assets and redirect unknown paths to `index.html` for client-side routing.\n  - Dependency Injection: Receives all necessary HTTP handlers, the WebSocket hub, and JWT secret in its constructor.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/storage/postgres/block_repository.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/storage/postgres/block_repository.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements the `BlockRepository` interface, providing PostgreSQL-specific data access logic for user blocking.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `BlockRepository`: Implements `repository.BlockRepository`, holding a database connection pool.\n      - Fields: `db` (*pgxpool.Pool).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewBlockRepository(db *pgxpool.Pool) *BlockRepository`: Public constructor for `BlockRepository`.\n    - `Save(ctx context.Context, blockerID, blockedID int64) error`: Public method to record a user block, preventing duplicate entries with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`.\n    - `Delete(ctx context.Context, blockerID, blockedID int64) error`: Public method to remove a user block.\n    - `IsBlocked(ctx context.Context, userID1, userID2 int64) (bool, error)`: Public method to check if two users have blocked each other in either direction.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Repository Pattern: Provides an abstraction layer over database operations.\n  - PostgreSQL Interaction: Uses `pgxpool` for database queries.\n  - `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`: Used in `Save` to gracefully handle attempts to block an already blocked user.\n",
  "quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/storage/postgres/conversation_repository.go": "- File Path: `quikchat-backend/internal/adapter/storage/postgres/conversation_repository.go`\n- High-Level Purpose: Implements the `ConversationRepository` interface, providing PostgreSQL-specific data access logic for conversations and participants.\n- Definitions in the File:\n  - **Structs**:\n    - `ConversationRepository`: Implements `repository.ConversationRepository`, holding a database connection pool.\n      - Fields: `db` (*pgxpool.Pool).\n  - **Functions**:\n    - `NewConversationRepository(db *pgxpool.Pool) repository.ConversationRepository`: Public constructor for `ConversationRepository`.\n    - `Save(ctx context.Context, conversation *domain.Conversation) error`: Public method to insert a new conversation into the database.\n    - `AddParticipant(ctx context.Context, conversationID, userID int64) error`: Public method to add a user as a participant to a conversation, handling duplicates.\n    - `IsUserInConversation(ctx context.Context, userID, conversationID int64) (bool, error)`: Public method to check if a specific user is a participant in a given conversation.\n    - `FindParticipantIDs(ctx context.Context, conversationID int64) ([]int64, error)`: Public method to retrieve all user IDs participating in a conversation.\n- Notable Patterns or Logic:\n  - Repository Pattern: Provides an abstraction layer over database operations.\n  - PostgreSQL Interaction: Uses `pgxpool` for database queries.\n  - `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`: Used in `AddParticipant` to prevent duplicate entries.\n",
